{"cuisine/chinese-imperial-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/chinese-imperial-cuisine","n":"Chinese imperial cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Beijing cuisine","cuisine/beijing-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in China","intro":[["Chinese imperial cuisine is derived from a variety of cooking styles of the regions in China, mainly from the cuisines of Shandong and Jiangsu provinces. The style originated from various Emperors' Kitchen and the Empress Dowagers' Kitchen, and it is similar to Beijing cuisine which it heavily influenced."],["Imperial cuisine was served mainly to the emperors, their empresses and concubines, and the imperial family. The characteristics of the Chinese imperial cuisine are the elaborate cooking methods and the strict selection of raw materials, which are often extremely expensive, rare or complicated in preparation. Visual presentation is also very important, so the colour and the shape of the dish must be carefully arranged. The most famous Chinese imperial cuisine restaurants are both located in Beijing: Fang Shan in Beihai Park and Ting Li Ting (聽鸝廳; tīng lí tīng) in the Summer Palace."],["Styles and tastes of Chinese imperial cuisine vary from dynasty to dynasty. Every dynasty has its own distinguishing features. The two famous styles of Chinese imperial cuisine are from the Ming and Qing dynasties. Many famous dishes emerged in these dynasties, such as Wensi tofu and Peking duck."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"chinese-imperial-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_imperial_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Beijing cuisine","cuisine/beijing-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Food for the Emperor: Recipes of Imperial China with a Dictionary of Chinese Cuisine","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"John D. Keys","note":"Older English collection of recipes associated with the imperial kitchens, with a glossary of Chinese culinary terms.","url":"","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Beijing cuisine","cuisine/beijing-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/5/5e/Manchu_Han_Imperial_Feast_Tao_Heung_Museum_of_Food_Culture.jpg/500px-Manchu_Han_Imperial_Feast_Tao_Heung_Museum_of_Food_Culture.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Cara Chow (Charlotte1125)","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manchu_Han_Imperial_Feast_Tao_Heung_Museum_of_Food_Culture.jpg"}},"cuisine/shanxi-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/shanxi-cuisine","n":"Shanxi cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in China","intro":[["Shanxi cuisine, or Jin cuisine, is derived from the native cooking styles of Shanxi Province in China. Local dishes include noodles, fried flatbread (da bing), and vinegar. Sour tastes are typical."],["Shanxi cuisine is not well known outside the region, partially due to its lower population compared to other provinces."],["Pork, chicken and lamb are among the most common meats—goat, and sheep offal are also often used. For example, lamb soup is usually cooked with livers, stomach and other offal. Lamb is used in Shanxi in ways that combines the cuisines of Northern Chinese minorities and Han Chinese. For example, using ground lamb and carrots as a dumpling filling, is something that is not found in any other Chinese cooking styles. Ground pork with chopped dill is another popular dumpling filling."],["The main staples reflect the crops commonly grown in Shanxi: millet, sorghum, and wheat. Pork, mushrooms, potatoes and turnips are frequently used."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"shanxi-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shanxi_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/1/16/Datong_Fideos.jpg/500px-Datong_Fideos.jpg","w":500,"h":667,"by":"Seba Della y Sole Bossio from Cordoba, Argentina","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Datong_Fideos.jpg"}},"dish/algerienne-sauce":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/algerienne-sauce","n":"Algérienne sauce","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Belgian cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Algérienne sauce or Algerian sauce is a sweet and spicy sauce with a yellowish-orange color. It is often available at fast-food businesses serving French tacos or kebabs, as well as friteries and other similar fast-food businesses, in Algeria, Tunisia, Morocco, Belgium, France, Switzerland, Japan and Québec."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-algerienne-sauce"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alg%C3%A9rienne_sauce"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Belgian cuisine","cuisine/belgian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Harissa","ingredient/harissa"],["Mayonnaise","ingredient/mayonnaise"]]},{"h":"More from Belgian cuisine","links":[["Belgian chocolate","dish/belgian-chocolate"],["Bickyburger","dish/bickyburger"],["Boûkète","dish/boukete"],["Flemish stew","dish/flemish-stew"],["Friterie","dish/friterie"],["Liège waffle","dish/liege-waffle"],["Moules-frites","dish/moules-frites"],["Paling in 't groen","dish/paling-in-t-groen"],["Samurai sauce","dish/samurai-sauce"],["Speculoos","dish/speculoos"],["Steak tartare","dish/steak-tartare"],["Waterzooi","dish/waterzooi"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/7/72/Alg%C3%A9rienne_sauce_from_O%27Tacos.jpg/500px-Alg%C3%A9rienne_sauce_from_O%27Tacos.jpg","w":500,"h":500,"by":"The wub","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alg%C3%A9rienne_sauce_from_O'Tacos.jpg"}},"dish/butter-chicken":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/butter-chicken","n":"Butter chicken","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Indian cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Butter chicken or murgh makhani is a curry made from chicken cooked in a spiced tomato and butter-based (makhani) gravy. The gravy is known for its creamy, rich texture. It is similar to chicken tikka masala, which uses a tomato paste. The dish was invented by Indian chefs Kundan Lal Gujral and Kundan Lal Jaggi and is associated with their Moti Mahal restaurant in Daryaganj, Delhi which popularized it in the 1950s. It has since become a staple cuisine of the city."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-butter-chicken"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Butter_chicken"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Indian cuisine","cuisine/indian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":5,"links":[["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Garam masala","ingredient/garam-masala"],["Kasuri methi","ingredient/kasuri-methi"],["Tomatoes","ingredient/tomatoes"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Swasthi's Recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"indianhealthyrecipes.com","note":"Swasthi Shreekanth's long-running blog of everyday Indian home cooking from across the regions.","url":"https://www.indianhealthyrecipes.com/about/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Hari Ghotra","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"harighotra.co.uk","note":"UK-based cook Hari Ghotra's recipe library and videos of home-style Indian dishes.","url":"https://www.harighotra.co.uk/indian-recipes","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"More from Indian cuisine","links":[["Akuri","dish/akuri"],["Aloo paratha","dish/aloo-paratha"],["Amba (condiment)","dish/amba-condiment"],["Baingan bharta","dish/baingan-bharta"],["Bombay potato","dish/bombay-potato"],["Chaat masala","dish/chaat-masala"],["Chicken Chettinad","dish/chicken-chettinad"],["Dahi vada","dish/dahi-vada"],["Dal dhokli","dish/dal-dhokli"],["Dal makhani","dish/dal-makhani"],["Fafda","dish/fafda"],["Falooda","dish/falooda"],["Fish and chips","dish/fish-and-chips"],["Handvo","dish/handvo"],["Hyderabadi haleem","dish/hyderabadi-haleem"],["Idli","dish/idli"],["Indian restaurant","dish/indian-restaurant"],["Kachumber","dish/kachumber"],["Kidyo","dish/kidyo"],["Kulfi","dish/kulfi"],["Masala chai","dish/masala-chai"],["Mirchi bada","dish/mirchi-bada"],["Mokthuk","dish/mokthuk"],["Naan","dish/naan"],["Osmania biscuit","dish/osmania-biscuit"],["Panipuri","dish/panipuri"],["Pathia","dish/pathia"],["Rabri","dish/rabri"],["Shemai","dish/shemai"],["Tandoori chicken","dish/tandoori-chicken"],["Tandoori roti","dish/tandoori-roti"],["Thali","dish/thali"],["Zarda (food)","dish/zarda-food"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/3/3c/Chicken_makhani.jpg/500px-Chicken_makhani.jpg","w":500,"h":332,"by":"stu_spivack","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chicken_makhani.jpg"}},"dish/dongpo-pork":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/dongpo-pork","n":"Dongpo pork","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Chinese cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Dongpo pork, also known as Dongpo meat, is a Hangzhou dish made by pan-frying and then red-cooking pork belly. The pork is typically cut into thick, approximately five-centimetre (two-inch) squares, with an even distribution of fat and lean meat, whilst retaining the skin. The texture is tender and juicy without being excessively greasy, accompanied by a fragrant aroma of wine. The dish is named in honor of Su Dongpo (Su Shi), a distinguished Song Dynasty poet and gastronome."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-dongpo-pork"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dongpo_pork"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Pork belly","ingredient/pork-belly"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"The Woks of Life","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"thewoksoflife.com","note":"Chinese and Chinese American recipes from the Leung family, running since 2013.","url":"https://thewoksoflife.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Chinese Cooking Demystified","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"youtube.com","note":"Technique-heavy YouTube channel on regional Chinese home and restaurant cooking.","url":"https://www.youtube.com/@ChineseCookingDemystified","suggested":true,"kind":"youtube"},{"name":"Red House Spice","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"redhousespice.com","note":"Classic Chinese recipes from Wei Guo, who grew up in Gansu, blogging since 2017.","url":"https://redhousespice.com/about/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Chinese cuisine","links":[["Almond biscuit","dish/almond-biscuit"],["Banana roll","dish/banana-roll"],["Baozi","dish/baozi"],["Bear paw","dish/bear-paw"],["Black sesame roll","dish/black-sesame-roll"],["Century egg","dish/century-egg"],["Cha siu bao","dish/cha-siu-bao"],["Chinese restaurant","dish/chinese-restaurant"],["Dandan noodles","dish/dandan-noodles"],["Dapanji","dish/dapanji"],["Dong'an chicken","dish/dong-an-chicken"],["Donkey burger","dish/donkey-burger"],["Doujiang","dish/doujiang"],["Drunken shrimp","dish/drunken-shrimp"],["Edible bird's nest","dish/edible-bird-s-nest"],["Fuqi feipian","dish/fuqi-feipian"],["Gaifan","dish/gaifan"],["Goubuli","dish/goubuli"],["Hainanese chicken rice","dish/hainanese-chicken-rice"],["Hog maw","dish/hog-maw"],["Hong dou tang","dish/hong-dou-tang"],["Hot dry noodles","dish/hot-dry-noodles"],["Hot pot","dish/hot-pot"],["Huangqiao sesame cake","dish/huangqiao-sesame-cake"],["Imitation shark fin soup","dish/imitation-shark-fin-soup"],["Jiaozi","dish/jiaozi"],["Jiuqu","dish/jiuqu"],["Kung Pao chicken","dish/kung-pao-chicken"],["Lanzhou beef noodles","dish/lanzhou-beef-noodles"],["Liangfen","dish/liangfen"],["Lo mai chi","dish/lo-mai-chi"],["Mao xue wang","dish/mao-xue-wang"],["Mapo tofu","dish/mapo-tofu"],["Mianwo","dish/mianwo"],["Miso soup","dish/miso-soup"],["Orange cuttlefish","dish/orange-cuttlefish"],["Osmanthus cake","dish/osmanthus-cake"],["Pickled egg","dish/pickled-egg"],["Pilaf","dish/pilaf"],["Red braised pork belly","dish/red-braised-pork-belly"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/0/0b/%E7%9B%90%E5%AE%98%E7%BC%B8%E8%82%892.jpg/500px-%E7%9B%90%E5%AE%98%E7%BC%B8%E8%82%892.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"三猎","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E7%9B%90%E5%AE%98%E7%BC%B8%E8%82%892.jpg"}},"dish/izakaya":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/izakaya","n":"Izakaya","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Japanese cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["An izakaya (Japanese: [izakaja]) is a type of informal Japanese bar that serves alcoholic drinks and snacks. Izakaya are casual places for after-work drinking, similar to a pub, a Spanish tapas bar, or an American saloon or tavern. Several varieties of izakaya exist throughout Japan that serve a larger clientele than traditional izakaya or pubs. Typically, izakaya serve fried or grilled food designed to be shared with multiple people."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-izakaya"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Izakaya"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Just One Cookbook","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"justonecookbook.com","note":"Namiko Chen's long-running blog and videos on Japanese home cooking.","url":"https://justonecookbook.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"RecipeTin Japan","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"japan.recipetineats.com","note":"Japanese home recipes from Yumiko, a Japan-born cook, sister site to RecipeTin Eats.","url":"https://japan.recipetineats.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Japanese cuisine","links":[["Bergh–Stoutenburgh House","dish/bergh-stoutenburgh-house"],["Chikuwa","dish/chikuwa"],["Curry bread","dish/curry-bread"],["Donburi","dish/donburi"],["Fukamushicha","dish/fukamushicha"],["Gyokuro","dish/gyokuro"],["Hitsumabushi","dish/hitsumabushi"],["Horumonyaki","dish/horumonyaki"],["Iekei ramen","dish/iekei-ramen"],["Inago no tsukudani","dish/inago-no-tsukudani"],["Itamae","dish/itamae"],["Japanese cheesecake","dish/japanese-cheesecake"],["Japanese noodles","dish/japanese-noodles"],["Japanese restaurant","dish/japanese-restaurant"],["Japanese tea","dish/japanese-tea"],["Jingisukan","dish/jingisukan"],["Kaki furai","dish/kaki-furai"],["Kamaboko","dish/kamaboko"],["Kōji (food)","dish/koji-food"],["Kusa mochi","dish/kusa-mochi"],["Kushikatsu","dish/kushikatsu"],["List of Japanese restaurants","dish/list-of-japanese-restaurants"],["Makizushi","dish/makizushi"],["Miso","dish/miso"],["Miso soup","dish/miso-soup"],["Monjayaki","dish/monjayaki"],["Nikujaga","dish/nikujaga"],["Nimono","dish/nimono"],["Oden","dish/oden"],["Onigiri","dish/onigiri"],["Osechi","dish/osechi"],["Oyakodon","dish/oyakodon"],["Rainbow roll","dish/rainbow-roll"],["Ramen","dish/ramen"],["Ryōtei","dish/ryotei"],["Sashimi","dish/sashimi"],["Sencha","dish/sencha"],["Shizuoka oden","dish/shizuoka-oden"],["Sumiso","dish/sumiso"],["Sushi","dish/sushi"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/b/b3/Izakaya_Exterior_Gotanda.jpg/500px-Izakaya_Exterior_Gotanda.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Japanexperterna","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Izakaya_Exterior_Gotanda.jpg"}},"ingredient/rye-bread":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/rye-bread","n":"Rye bread","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 4 dishes across 11 cuisines","intro":[["Rye bread is a type of bread made with various proportions of flour from rye grain. It can be light or dark in color, depending on the type of flour used and the addition of coloring agents, and is typically denser than bread made from wheat flour. Compared to white bread, it is higher in fiber, darker in color, and stronger in flavor. The world's largest exporter of rye bread is Poland."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-rye-bread"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rye_bread"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":11,"groups":[["Estonian cuisine","cuisine/estonian-cuisine",[["Kvass","dish/kvass"],["Smörgåstårta","dish/smorgastarta"]]],["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Reuben sandwich","dish/reuben-sandwich"]]],["Finnish cuisine","cuisine/finnish-cuisine",[["Smörgåstårta","dish/smorgastarta"]]],["Georgian cuisine","cuisine/georgian-cuisine",[["Kvass","dish/kvass"]]],["Icelandic cuisine","cuisine/icelandic-cuisine",[["Smörgåstårta","dish/smorgastarta"]]],["Jewish cuisine","cuisine/jewish-cuisine",[["Reuben sandwich","dish/reuben-sandwich"]]],["Latvian cuisine","cuisine/latvian-cuisine",[["Kvass","dish/kvass"]]],["Lithuanian cuisine","cuisine/lithuanian-cuisine",[["Kvass","dish/kvass"]]],["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine",[["Karelian pasty","dish/karelian-pasty"]]],["Swedish cuisine","cuisine/swedish-cuisine",[["Smörgåstårta","dish/smorgastarta"]]],["Ukrainian cuisine","cuisine/ukrainian-cuisine",[["Kvass","dish/kvass"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Rye flour","ingredient/rye-flour"],["Barley","ingredient/barley"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Hordeum","ingredient/hordeum"],["Millet","ingredient/millet"],["Pearl barley","ingredient/pearl-barley"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Potatoes","ingredient/potatoes"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Rice pudding","ingredient/rice-pudding"],["Rye","ingredient/rye"],["Smetana","ingredient/smetana"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Malt","ingredient/malt"],["Yeast","ingredient/yeast"],["Corned beef","ingredient/corned-beef"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/d/d2/Market_Bread%2C_Riga%2C_Latvia.jpg/500px-Market_Bread%2C_Riga%2C_Latvia.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Ken Eckert","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Market_Bread%2C_Riga%2C_Latvia.jpg"}},"dish/kesakeitto":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/kesakeitto","n":"Kesäkeitto","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Finnish cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Kesäkeitto is a traditional vegetable soup in Finnish cuisine cooked in milk with butter, potatoes, carrots, peas, cauliflower and possibly other vegetables of the season. During the wars in the 1940s, summer soup was very popular in Finland because the availability of many foods was inconsistent, it was satisfying, and it could be made with ingredients from the garden. For this reason, the Finno-Swedes called it snålsoppa (snål means \"stingy\")."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-kesakeitto"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kes%C3%A4keitto"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Finnish cuisine","cuisine/finnish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":5,"links":[["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Cauliflower","ingredient/cauliflower"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Scallion","ingredient/scallion"]]},{"h":"More from Finnish cuisine","links":[["Blodpalt","dish/blodpalt"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Egg butter","dish/egg-butter"],["Glögg","dish/glogg"],["Joulutorttu","dish/joulutorttu"],["Kalakukko","dish/kalakukko"],["Karelian hot pot","dish/karelian-hot-pot"],["Kringle","dish/kringle"],["Kuivaliha","dish/kuivaliha"],["Lanttulaatikko","dish/lanttulaatikko"],["Lohikeitto","dish/lohikeitto"],["Lörtsy","dish/lortsy"],["Semla","dish/semla"],["Smörgåstårta","dish/smorgastarta"],["Strong beer","dish/strong-beer"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/c/ca/Kes%C3%A4keitto_DSC04420_C.JPG/500px-Kes%C3%A4keitto_DSC04420_C.JPG","w":500,"h":491,"by":"Anneli Salo","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kes%C3%A4keitto_DSC04420_C.JPG"}},"ingredient/palapa-condiment":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/palapa-condiment","n":"Palapa (condiment)","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 3 cuisines","intro":[["Palapa is a sweet and spicy Filipino condiment made from thinly chopped white scallions (sakurab), pounded ginger (luya pagirison), turmeric (kalawag), labuyo chili (luya tiduk), and toasted grated coconut (niog). It originates from the Maranao people of Lanao del Sur. The ingredients are mixed together and cooked briefly or cooked until slightly dry. It is stored immediately in sealed jars (garapon) after cooking. Palapa can be used as an ingredient in certain dishes, most notably in piaparan, or as a condiment after briefly sautéing, often with a spoonful of condensed milk. It can also be eaten fresh as salad dressing. Palapa is an important cultural symbol of the Maranao people and is a common accompaniment at every meal."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-palapa-condiment"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palapa_(condiment)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["Filipino cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine",[["Piaparan","dish/piaparan"]]],["Mindanao cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine-mindanao",[["Piaparan","dish/piaparan"]]],["Visayan cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine-visayas",[["Piaparan","dish/piaparan"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Turmeric","ingredient/turmeric"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/6/6d/Palapa_%28Philippines%29.jpg/500px-Palapa_%28Philippines%29.jpg","w":500,"h":358,"by":"Obsidian Soul","lic":"CC0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palapa_(Philippines).jpg"}},"ingredient/almond-paste":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/almond-paste","n":"Almond paste","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 4 cuisines","intro":[["Almond paste is made from ground almonds or almond meal and sugar in equal quantities, with small amounts of cooking oil, eggs, heavy cream or corn syrup added as a binder. It is similar to marzipan, with a coarser texture. Almond paste is used as a filling in pastries, but it can also be found in chocolates. In a type of commercially manufactured almond paste called persipan, ground apricot or peach pits are added to save money."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-almond-paste"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Almond_paste"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":4,"groups":[["Finnish cuisine","cuisine/finnish-cuisine",[["Semla","dish/semla"]]],["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine",[["Pithivier","dish/pithivier"]]],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine",[["Pithivier","dish/pithivier"]]],["Swedish cuisine","cuisine/swedish-cuisine",[["Semla","dish/semla"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Frangipane","ingredient/frangipane"],["Puff pastry","ingredient/puff-pastry"],["Jam","ingredient/jam"],["Wheat bread","ingredient/wheat-bread"],["Whipped cream","ingredient/whipped-cream"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/8/85/Almond_paste_%28Amandelspijs%29.jpg/500px-Almond_paste_%28Amandelspijs%29.jpg","w":500,"h":330,"by":"Rasbak","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Almond_paste_(Amandelspijs).jpg"}},"dis